WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.390 --> 00:00:04.020 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  my ride back from work on June 3rd 2017  was not your typical commute I was in  the backseat of a white pickup truck  with temperatures outside edging up to  115 degrees the roof of our car was  painted with a humanitarian symbol so  that we wouldn't be hit by an air strike  that summer I was an aid worker in  northern Iraq during the final stages of  the operation against Isis in Mosul  after a 10-hour day in the fields  covered in dust all I wanted was to get  home and take a cold shower  but the day wasn't over yet as my  colleagues and I made her way home we  started hearing reports that  neighborhoods in Mosul were filling with  poisonous gas Twitter was lighting up  with these cell phone images showing  aircraft firing white sprays of smoke  into the city it was quickly identified  as white phosphorus used by the us-led  coalition and the Iraqi army white  phosphorus can be used legitimately and  more as a smokescreen to obscure  military movements but when humans come  into contact with it it can also burn  flesh off of the bone so depending on  how it's used it can either be a totally  legitimate tactic of war or war crime  and sometimes it's nearly impossible to  tell the difference my colleagues began  to argue should we demand an  international outcry for the for the  coalition's use of chemicals in a  densely populated city or given that  it's a gray area should we focus energy  on other problems it was a judgment call  a decision made by a handful of  well-meaning humanitarians we decided  not to push it  I was left confused by the whole  incident how could something as weighty  as a war crime be so slippery so hard to  pin down back in the States  I began studying war crimes and I  quickly learned that there's no clear  answer and the technology of war is  evolving so quickly the question is only  becoming more urgent the concept of war  crimes originates in the earliest eras  of human history ancient codes of war  from China India and Greece regulated  troops conduct today the laws of war  provide guidance on who qualifies as  combatants the limits of the  battlefields and what sorts of acts are  permissible troops must wear uniforms  they should avoid civilians they can't  torture prisoners things like that when  you think about it it's actually kind of  bizarre acts that would ordinarily land  you in jail like taking someone's life  or destroying a building are totally  legal if you're at war but even in war  there's a category of actions that  humanity has decided is beyond the pale  and those we call war crimes  we even have tribunals that attempt to  bring perpetrators to justice like  following the Rwandan genocide or ethnic  cleansing in the Balkans to me there's  something profound about humans  attempting to bring integrity and ethics  into battle but in practice it's not so  easy to draw the line partially because  war is really messy and chaotic but also  because it turns out there is no clear  universal definition of a war crime ask  any international lawyer and you'll get  this circular answer a war crime is a  serious violation of the laws of war  what's a serious violation of the laws  of war a war crime  as a result historically courts have  sometimes struggled to decide is  terrorism a war crime is rape is  recruiting child soldiers if it seems  confusing now it's about to get a whole  lot worse technology being developed  right here in Silicon Valley  artificial intelligence autonomous  vehicles drones is the same technology  that will dictate the future of war and  it's evolving so rapidly it's already  outpaced the law we're headed for a  legal black hole where anything goes  in particular technology is complicating  the three areas where law used to  provide clarity who is like who are the  combatants where is the battlefield and  what are the harms first who are the  combatants picture a fully autonomous AI  powered weapon system to be clear this  doesn't exist yet but it's not  inconceivable it's been pre-programmed  to strike enemy targets without human  intervention it may actually be more  precise and accurate less less prone to  error but it's imperfect and let's say  it's somehow commits a horrendous act  like targeting a hospital instead of a  military base  what can we hold responsible technology  creates a massive accountability gap war  crimes are committed by combatants but  our computer programmers and  manufacturers combatant unless it could  be proven that they were somehow aware  of a defect they're simply too far  removed from the crime and would it even  be fair to charge an engineer in Palo  Alto with war crimes committed halfway  around the globe second where is the  battlefield imagine a nuclear explosion  in space creates an electromagnetic  pulse that reaches Earth's atmosphere  causing aircraft to fall from the sky or  imagine a cyber attack launched from a  suburban living room  that wipes out a power grid in another  country the law of work tries to keep  destruction on the battlefields but  there's no standalone regulation of  warfare in space and there's no  consensus on the limits of cyber war  words of the future will rarely look  like two armies meeting on the  battlefields war will be fought from  remote locations and it will be carried  out in outer space  and in digital space and third what are  the harms the law of war usually  conceives of harm in terms of physical  damage violence bloodshed destruction  but new weapons will cause different  harms like environmental damage  imagine a weapon leaves behind toxic  remnants that slowly destroys farmland  leaving it inhospitable or imagine a  cyberattack that destroys information  medical records financial data we  haven't yet decided if those sorts of  harms should be off limit and war does a  war crime require physical pain physical  destruction these questions are not  easily answered the law of war was  created before artificial intelligence  even before the Internet  we're retrofitting rules from the 19th  and 20th century to a new world  and frankly these are questions that no  one wants to think about the latest  version of the United States official  guidelines on autonomous weapons is no  more than a couple page PDF document  with track changes left on that's  terrifying some say it's not worth  establishing legal guidelines if you're  not sure that we can hold people  accountable anyway  some say that regulation will stifle  innovation I say it's a cop-out I don't  have all the answers but my instinct is  that war is and should remain a uniquely  human endeavor there should be  meaningful human control over life and  death decisions for sure technology can  do a lot to improve defense  and reduce risk for servicemembers and  civilians we can never fully delegate  moral agency to a machine and we should  resist the temptation to try at Stanford  were tasked with thinking up the  unimaginable the new business idea the  moonshot invention the sobering side of  that mandate is the duty to also  consider the harms what the adverse  actor might do if given a chance or the  well-meaning actor who simply has no  legal guidance this isn't limited to  warfare ethics should be a central  component of all technology design not  an afterthought the mere act of writing  down standards helps to build a  consensus on what's acceptable and what  isn't we can't trust that existing law  has all of the answers as is clear with  war crimes it may have very few war is a  tragic reality but the decisions we make  today can ensure that it is constrained  to the battlefields and the soldiers who  fight on it thank you  [Applause]  [Music]

